889:
Except as may be specifically permitted hereinafter, it shall be unlawful for any person to erect, place or use within the city, when visible from any public way, any of the following signs:
(1) Snipe signs;
(2) A sign which contains any moving, flashing, animated lights, visible moving or moveable parts, or giving the appearance of animation;
(3) Roof signs;
(4) Any sign which emits a sound, odor or visible matter;
(5) Any sign which obstructs free ingress to or egress from a required door, window, fire escape or other required exit way;
(6) Any sign and/or sign structure which obstructs the view of, may be confused with, or purposed to be a governmental or traffic directional/safety sign;
(7) Any sign or sign structure, other than freestanding, which extends above the parapet or building roofline against which the sign is located;
(8) Signs using the words "stop," "danger" or any other word, phrase, symbol or character in a manner that misleads, confuses or distracts a vehicle driver;
(9) Moving message signs;
(10) Except as otherwise provided, no sign, whether temporary or permanent, except by a public agency, is permitted within any street or highway right-of-way;
(11) Signs painted on or attached to trees, fence posts, rocks or other natural features, telephone or utility poles or painted on the roofs of buildings visible from any public way;
(12) Abandoned or dilapidated signs;
(13) Any sign which exhibits statements, words or pictures of obscene or pornographic subjects or material;
(14) Portable signs;
(15) Inflatable signs;
(16) Finger or pennant streamers;
(17) Paper signs or paper advertising on the exterior of building or on the interior of a window or door, or within three feet of a window or door, so as to be visible from the outside; or
(18) Reserved.
(19) A vehicle sign, when the vehicle is (a) not regularly used in the conduct of the business or activity advertised on the vehicle, and (b) is visible from a public right-of-way. A vehicle shall not be considered as "regularly used in the conduct of the business or activity" if the vehicle is used primarily (i) for advertising, or (ii) for the purpose of advertising.
(Ord. 05-01-01, 2-15-05; Ord. No. 10-01-02, 1-19-10; Ord. No. 10-08-01, 8-17-10; Ord. No. 14-01-01, 2-18-14; Ord. No. 16-06-01, 6-21-16)

907:
(a) Where to apply. Any application for a sign permit shall be submitted to the city manager on a form supplied by the city.
(b) Information required. The following information shall be submitted with the application for a sign permit:
(1) Name, address, telephone number and city occupation tax license number of sign permittee;
(2) Legal description and/or street address of premises or property upon which the sign is to be located;
(3) The type of sign for which a permit is being sought;
(4) An exact color rendering, to scale, of the proposed sign showing elevations of the sign, and the relationship to other existing adjacent signs. In the case of a freestanding sign, the rendering shall include a site plan showing the sign location and any existing or proposed landscaping which is affected by each sign;
(5) If required by the planning and design review board, a copy of stress sheets and calculations indicating that the sign is properly designed for dead load and wind pressure in any direction, as required in chapter 12 of the Standard Building Code as adopted by the city.
(Ord. No. 05-01-01, 2-15-05)

937:
(a) The building inspector may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial, or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building, or structure; of the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings, or structures; or of other residents of the city. Such conditions may include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects; and
(6) Uncleanliness.
(b) The building inspector may determine, under existing ordinances, that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon the personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Ord. No. 96-08-03, 9-17-96)

938:
The building inspector is authorized to exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purpose and provisions of this article, including, without limitation, the following:
(1) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) To appoint and fix the duties of such officer, agents, and employees as he deems necessary to carry out the purposes of the ordinances; and
(5) To delegate any of his functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as he may designate.
(Ord. No. 96-08-03, 9-17-96)

990:
A person commits the offense of disorderly conduct in the city when such person commits any of the following:
(1) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another whereby any person is placed in fear of the safety of such person's life, limb or health;
(2) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another whereby the property of any person is placed in danger of being damaged or destroyed;
(3) Causes, provoke, or engage in any fight, brawl, or riotous conduct to endanger the life, limb, health, or property of another;
(4) Interferes, by acts of physical obstruction, with another's pursuit of lawful occupation;
(5) Throws bottles, paper, cans, glass, sticks, stones, or any other debris on any public property;
(6) Directs fighting words toward another, that is, words which by their very nature tend to incite an immediate breach of the peace;
(7) Stands or remain in or about any street, sidewalk, overpass or public way so as to impede the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic, and to fail to clear such street, sidewalk, overpass or public way after being ordered to do so by a police officer or other lawful authority;
(8) Without provocation, uses to or of another person in such other person's presence, opprobrious or abusive words which by their very utterance tend to incite to an immediate breach of the peace, that is to say, words which as a matter of common knowledge and under ordinary circumstances will, when used to or of another person in such other person's presence, naturally tend to provoke violent resentment, that is, words commonly called "fighting words"; or
(9) Without provocation, uses obscene and vulgar or profane language in the presence of or by telephone to a person under the age of 14 years which threatens an immediate breach of the peace.
(Ord. No. 97-08-01, 9-16-97; Ord. No. 22-11-02, 12-13-22)

1001:
(a) A person who commits any of the following acts in a public transit bus, a public transit vehicle, a cable car, a trolley, a rail car, or a station shall be guilty of a misdemeanor:
(1) Spits;
(2) Discards litter, except into receptacles designated for that purpose;
(3) Plays any radio; cassette, cartridge, or tape player; or similar device unless such device is connected to an earphone that limits the sound to the hearing of the individual user;
(4) Carries or possesses any explosives, acids, other dangerous articles, or live animals, except for seeing eye dogs properly harnessed and accompanied by a blind person and small animals properly packaged;
(5) Obstructs, hinders, interferes with, or otherwise disrupts or disturbs the operation or operator of a public transit bus, a public transit vehicle, a cable car, a trolley, or a rail car; or
(6) Boards any public transit bus, a public transit vehicle, a cable car, a trolley, or a rail car through an exit door, unless so directed by an employee or agent of the carrier.
(b) A person convicted of a first offense of violating subsection (a) of this Code section shall be punished by a fine of not less than $50.00 and not more than $100.00. Upon a second or subsequent conviction, a person shall be punished by a fine of not less than $100.00 and not more than $250.00 or by imprisonment for not more than ten days, or both.
(Ord. No. 97-08-01, 9-16-97)

1022:
(a) This division shall not apply to any person under 18 years of age who:
(1) Is or has been emancipated by marriage;
(2) Is accompanied by his/her parent, parents, legal guardian or other adult having responsibility for lawful custody and care of the minor;
(3) Is engaged in employment or employment-related activities;
(4) Is engaged in interstate or intrastate travel;
(5) Is attending school, a religious or civic organization function that is supervised;
(6) Is attending or traveling directly to or from an activity involving the exercise of First Amendment rights of free speech, freedom of assembly, or free exercise of religion;
(7) Is in a public place because of an emergency;
(8) Is on the sidewalk in front of his or her home or an adjacent dwelling; and
(9) Is on an errand directed by his or her parent, parents, legal guardian or other adult person having responsibility for the lawful care and custody of the minor.
(b) Business owners also commit a violation of this division if they allow a minor to remain on the premises of an establishment defined herein during curfew hours. It shall be a defense or an exception for the business owner if the business owner, the agent, or employee of the business owner, notified the city police about the minor remaining on the business premises after he or she had been requested to leave.
(Ord. No. 95-02-01, 7-18-95)

1109:
The following activities within the Market Platz are prohibited:
(1) Open flames;
(2) Dispersing of any liquids;
(3) Unreasonable obstruction of pedestrian traffic to businesses;
(4) Any electronically amplified sound except when originating from the band shell or speakers provided by the city;
(5) Use of profanity; and
(6) Consumption of alcohol or display of alcoholic beverages.
(7) Use of any animal or bird to perform in the Market Platz area which may leave excrement causing unsanitary conditions, littering of the sidewalks or causing damage to the landscaping or other city property; or the use of any animal or bird in a performance which can create a biting or scratching hazard to the public.
(8) Use of the city planters at the Market Platz area for any type of display or signage.
(Ord. No. 10-03-01, 4-6-10; Ord. No. 13-06-02, 7-16-13)
